// 031 — Long / arbitrary-precision integers — DESIGN NOTE
// When i64 (±9.22 * 10^18) runs out of room — 30! or a 4096-bit RSA
// modulus or Avogadro's number — the fix is arbitrary-precision
// arithmetic: an integer represented as an array of limbs (usually
// u32 or u64), operated on via long multiplication / division
// algorithms (Karatsuba, Toom-Cook, FFT for the really big ones).
//
// AiLang does not ship a BigInt type in core. When it arrives, it'll
// most likely live in a library (`Library.BigInt`) reached via
// `LibraryImport.BigInt`, with operations named BigAdd/BigMul/BigMod
// to keep the Integer primitive names undisturbed. The algorithms
// are well-known; the work is surface-level (printing, parsing,
// comparison shape) rather than mathematical.
//
// For now, "did I overflow?" is a question AiLang doesn't answer
// automatically — i64 silently wraps on arithmetic overflow the same
// way C does. Guard manually when input ranges make it possible.
SubRoutine.Main {
    // 20! fits in i64 (2432902008176640000). 21! does not.
    fact = 1
    i = 1
    WhileLoop LessEqual(i, 20) {
        fact = Multiply(fact, i)
        i = Add(i, 1)
    }
    PrintMessage("20! = ")
    PrintNumber(fact)
    PrintMessage(" (fits in i64)\n")
    PrintMessage("21! would overflow — would wrap silently.\n")
}
RunTask(Main)
